A = [
dict(n=10, slug="fine-tuning-lora", title="How Do You Teach an AI Something New?", topic="Pretraining, fine-tuning and LoRA",
buzz='"We\'ll fine-tune it." "Just use LoRA." Teams say these when an AI doesn\'t talk the way they want. What actually changes?',
hw='Think of a chef.\n\n**Pretraining** is culinary school: years of learning to cook everything.\n**Fine-tuning** is a short course in one cuisine, say South Indian, that changes how the chef cooks.\n**LoRA** is a recipe card clipped to the chef\'s apron: small, cheap, removable, and it changes a few dishes without retraining the chef.',
step='Pretraining builds the model from scratch on huge amounts of text. Very few companies do it.\n\nFine-tuning continues training on a smaller, focused set of examples, adjusting the model\'s knobs (write-up 8) towards your style or task.\n\nLoRA freezes the original knobs and trains a small add-on of extra knobs instead. It\'s much cheaper, and you can swap add-ons for different tasks.',
met='Company chatbots that answer in a fixed brand tone. Image apps that learn your face from a handful of photos often use a LoRA-style add-on.',
name='**Pretraining**, **fine-tuning** and **LoRA** (low-rank adaptation).',
words=[("Pretraining","the first, huge training run that builds the model."),("Fine-tuning","extra training on focused examples."),("LoRA","a small trainable add-on; the main model stays frozen.")],
notit='Fine-tuning is mainly for style, format and behaviour, not for adding fresh facts. For facts that change, looking them up at question time usually works better (#JustAddRAG).',
take=["Try better instructions and examples first; they cost nothing.","Fine-tune for how it should answer, not for what's new this week.","Ask whether a LoRA add-on would do before paying for full fine-tuning."],
close="School, a short course, a recipe card. Pick the smallest that works.",
q="Has your team ever fine-tuned when a better prompt would have done?",
scene='"Let\'s fine-tune the model."\nOn what, and why?',
hook="Pretraining is chef school. Fine-tuning is a short course. LoRA is a recipe card.",
extra="Start with the smallest change that works."),

dict(n=11, slug="rlhf", title="How Did the AI Learn to Be Polite?", topic="RLHF",
buzz='"RLHF." It\'s why chatbots answer helpfully instead of rambling. The acronym is a mouthful.',
hw='Ask a freshly trained AI "How do I boil an egg?" It might continue with three more questions, because it learned to continue text, not to answer.\n\nNow show people two answers and ask: which is better? 👍 or 👎. Do that thousands of times, and teach the AI to give more answers people prefer. That\'s the hello world: **learning from thumbs up and thumbs down.**',
step='First, people write example answers to teach the basic "question, then answer" habit. Then they compare pairs of AI answers and rank them. A second model learns to predict which answers people prefer, and the main AI is trained to score well with it.\n\nNewer methods skip the second model and learn from the comparisons directly, but the idea is the same: human preferences shape behaviour.',
met='The thumbs up and down buttons under chatbot answers. Chatbots that politely decline harmful requests.',
name='**RLHF**: reinforcement learning from human feedback.',
words=[("Human feedback","people choosing which answer is better."),("Reward model","a model that learns to predict those choices."),("Alignment","shaping an AI to be helpful, honest and safe.")],
notit='RLHF doesn\'t teach new facts, and it isn\'t a guarantee of truth. It teaches what answers people like, which can include sounding confident.',
take=["Know that \"helpful assistant\" behaviour was trained, not built in.","Use feedback buttons; they can shape future versions.","Remember: preferred isn't always correct. Check facts that matter."],
close="Thumbs up, thumbs down, thousands of times. That's how it learned manners.",
q="Do you click the thumbs up and down buttons?",
scene='A raw AI, asked "How do I boil an egg?",\nreplies with three more questions.',
hook="Thumbs up, thumbs down, thousands of times. That's how AIs learned to answer.",
extra="It's called RLHF."),

dict(n=12, slug="distillation", title="How Does a Small AI Learn From a Big One?", topic="Distillation",
buzz='"A distilled model." Small, fast AIs are often described this way. It sounds like whisky, and the idea isn\'t far off.',
hw='A senior doctor (the big AI) and a junior doctor (the small AI) see the same patients. The junior doesn\'t just learn the final diagnosis. They learn how the senior weighs every option: "probably flu, maybe a cold, unlikely anything worse".\n\nThat\'s the hello world: a small **student** model learns by copying a big **teacher** model\'s answers and reasoning.',
step='The teacher answers a large set of questions. The student is trained to match the teacher\'s outputs, including how likely it thought each option was, which carries more information than a single right answer.\n\nThe student ends up much smaller and cheaper, keeping much of the teacher\'s skill on the tasks it was taught.',
met='The "mini", "flash" or "lite" versions of popular models are often built this way. Fast on-device assistants too (write-up 20).',
name='**Distillation**, also called **knowledge distillation**.',
words=[("Teacher model","the big model being learned from."),("Student model","the small model doing the learning."),("Distilled model","the small model that results.")],
notit='The student isn\'t a full copy. It loses some skill, especially outside what it was taught. Also check licences: some providers forbid using their outputs to train other models.',
take=["Try the small, cheap version of a model before the big one.","Test it on your own hard cases; that's where students slip.","Check the licence before training on another model's outputs."],
close="A small student, taught by a big teacher.",
q="Have you tried a model's mini version?",
scene='A big AI is smart but slow and costly.\nA small AI is fast but weaker.',
hook="What if the big one taught the small one? That's distillation.",
extra="Many fast, cheap AIs are built this way."),

dict(n=13, slug="synthetic-data", title="Can an AI Learn From Data Another AI Wrote?", topic="Synthetic data",
buzz='"We trained on synthetic data." It sounds like fake data. In a way it is, on purpose.',
hw='A teacher needs 1,000 maths practice questions with worked answers. Writing them by hand would take months.\n\nSo an AI writes them, another check verifies the answers, and a new AI practises on them. That\'s the hello world: **training data written by an AI**, not collected from people.',
step='It\'s useful when real data is scarce, private or expensive: rare medical cases, new languages, tricky edge cases, or step-by-step reasoning examples.\n\nThe catch is quality. If an AI learns only from AI-written data, mistakes and blandness can pile up over generations. So teams check it, filter it, and mix it with real data.',
met='Practice questions in learning apps. Many newer models mention synthetic data in how they were trained, especially for maths and coding.',
name='**Synthetic data**.',
words=[("Synthetic data","data generated by a model or program, not collected."),("Data augmentation","making more examples by varying real ones."),("Model collapse","quality dropping when models train mostly on AI-made data.")],
notit='Synthetic doesn\'t mean worthless or free. Unchecked, it can teach errors confidently. It\'s a tool, not a replacement for real data.',
take=["Use synthetic data to fill gaps, not to replace real examples.","Always check a sample by hand.","Keep real-world test sets to catch drift."],
close="AI can write the homework. Someone still has to mark it.",
q="Would you trust a model trained on AI-written data?",
scene='You need 1,000 practice questions.\nWriting them would take months.',
hook="So an AI writes them, and another AI learns from them. That's synthetic data.",
extra="Useful, as long as someone checks the answers."),

dict(n=14, slug="scaling-laws", title="Why Did Bigger AI Keep Getting Better?", topic="Scaling laws",
buzz='"Scaling laws." The reason companies spend billions on bigger AIs. And lately, the reason people ask if it\'s slowing down.',
hw='Practise piano for 1 hour and you improve a lot. 10 hours, you improve more. 100 hours, still more, but each extra hour adds a little less.\n\nAI researchers found the same pattern: give a model more **data**, more **knobs** (write-up 8) and more **computing power**, and it gets predictably better. That\'s the hello world of **scaling laws**.',
step='The surprise was how predictable it was. Researchers could estimate how good a bigger model would be before building it, so companies kept scaling up.\n\nBut each step costs far more for a smaller gain, and good training text is limited. So the focus has shifted: better data, smarter training, and letting models think longer at answer time (write-up 21) instead of only getting bigger.',
met='News about record-sized AI data centres and chips. And headlines asking whether AI progress is hitting a wall.',
name='**Scaling laws**.',
words=[("Scaling","growing model size, data and compute together."),("Compute","the computing power used for training or answering."),("Diminishing returns","each extra unit helping a little less.")],
notit='Scaling laws aren\'t laws of nature. They\'re observed trends that held for years, and they don\'t promise the trend continues forever.',
take=["Expect progress from smarter methods, not just bigger models.","Don't assume the biggest model is the best value for your task.","Read \"it's slowing down\" headlines as \"the easy gains are used up\"."],
close="More practice helps. Each extra hour helps a little less.",
q="Do you think bigger AI will keep getting better?",
scene='1 hour of piano practice: big improvement.\n100 hours: still better, but each hour adds less.',
hook="AI improved the same way. Researchers call it scaling laws.",
extra="That's why companies built ever bigger models."),

dict(n=15, slug="quantization", title="How Do You Fit a Giant AI on a Laptop?", topic="Quantization",
buzz='"A 4-bit quantized model." It\'s how big AIs run on laptops and phones. It sounds like physics.',
hw='Take a photo and save it as a smaller file. It\'s a bit less sharp, but you can still see everything that matters, and it takes a fraction of the space.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **quantization** stores an AI\'s billions of knobs (write-up 8) with fewer digits each, so the whole model shrinks.',
step='Normally each knob is stored as a very precise number, like 0.73914. Quantization rounds it, say to 0.74, using fewer bits of memory. Going from 16 bits to 4 bits per knob makes the model roughly four times smaller and often faster.\n\nRound too hard and quality drops, like an over-compressed photo turning blocky. Good methods keep most of the quality.',
met='Running an AI on a laptop with apps like Ollama or LM Studio. Model downloads labelled "Q4" or "4-bit".',
name='**Quantization**.',
words=[("Quantization","storing a model's numbers with less precision."),("Bit","the basic unit of computer memory; fewer bits per number, smaller model."),("Precision","how many digits each number keeps.")],
notit='Quantization isn\'t free. Quality can slip, especially on maths, rare languages or long reasoning. Test the smaller version on your real tasks.',
take=["Use quantized models to run AI locally or cheaply.","Test quality on your hardest examples before switching.","Compare a bigger quantized model with a smaller full one; either can win."],
close="Same photo, smaller file. Same AI, smaller memory.",
q="Have you run an AI on your own laptop?",
scene='A giant AI needs a giant computer.\nYour laptop has a fraction of that memory.',
hook="Quantization is saving the same photo as a smaller file.",
extra="It's how big AIs fit on laptops and phones."),

dict(n=16, slug="mixture-of-experts", title="Why Doesn't the Whole AI Wake Up for Every Question?", topic="Mixture of experts",
buzz='"A mixture-of-experts model." Many of the newest big models use it. It sounds like a committee meeting.',
hw='Walk into a hospital with a broken arm. You don\'t see every doctor. A receptionist sends you to orthopaedics, and only those specialists get involved.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: a **mixture of experts** has many specialist parts and a router that sends each piece of text to only a few of them.',
step='For every token (write-up 2), a small router picks a handful of experts, often 2 out of 8, or a few out of hundreds. The others stay idle.\n\nSo a model can have a huge total size but use only a small part for each word. Mixtral 8x7B, for example, uses 2 of its 8 experts per token. It gets the knowledge of a big model at closer to the running cost of a small one.',
met='Several well-known open models (like Mixtral and DeepSeek) use this design, and many big commercial models are widely reported to.',
name='**Mixture of experts (MoE)**.',
words=[("Expert","one specialist part of the model."),("Router","the part that picks which experts handle each token."),("Active parameters","the knobs actually used for each token.")],
notit='The experts aren\'t neat topics like "maths" or "law". They specialise in patterns the model found on its own. And all experts still have to be loaded in memory.',
take=["Read \"total\" and \"active\" parameters separately when comparing models.","Expect MoE models to need lots of memory even if they're fast.","Judge them on your tasks, like any other model."],
close="Big hospital, but you only see the doctors you need.",
q="Did the hospital analogy make it click?",
scene='A broken arm. You don\'t see every doctor in the hospital.\nOnly the right specialists.',
hook="A mixture of experts sends each word to only a few specialists.",
extra="Big knowledge, smaller running cost."),

dict(n=17, slug="kv-cache", title="Why Doesn't the AI Re-Read Everything for Every Word?", topic="KV cache",
buzz='"KV cache." It\'s in every article about making AI faster and cheaper. It sounds like a database setting.',
hw='You\'re reading a book aloud and adding one word at a time. Would you start again from page 1 before each new word? Of course not. You remember what you\'ve read and just add the next word.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: the **KV cache** is the AI\'s notes on everything it has already read, so it doesn\'t redo the work for each new token (write-up 3).',
step='For every token, attention (write-up 5) works out some notes: what this word is about and how it relates to others. Without a cache, the AI would recalculate those notes for the whole text each time it writes one more word.\n\nWith the cache, it keeps the notes and only computes them for the new word. The cost: the notes take memory, and long chats mean big caches.',
met='Long answers that keep streaming smoothly. "Prompt caching" discounts from AI providers build on the same idea of reusing work (cost is covered in Series 8).',
name='The **KV cache** (key-value cache).',
words=[("Cache","saved work you can reuse instead of redoing."),("Key and value","the two kinds of notes attention keeps for each token."),("Prompt caching","reusing the cache for a repeated start of a prompt.")],
notit='It\'s not memory of you or past chats. The cache lasts only while one answer (or one cached prompt) is being processed.',
take=["Expect long chats to use more memory, partly because of the cache.","Put the fixed part of your prompt first, so providers can reuse it.","Know that \"faster serving\" headlines often mean smarter caching."],
close="Don't reread the whole book for every new word.",
q="Did you know AIs keep notes while they write?",
scene='Reading a book aloud, one word at a time.\nYou don\'t restart from page 1 before every word.',
hook="Neither does an AI. It keeps notes, called the KV cache.",
extra="That's how long answers stay fast."),
]
